This was based on the 3.15 PR: https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/pull/43129/files

Fixes issue dotnet/runtime#57472

Main pr dotnet/runtime#57479

Also backported to 5.0 in dotnet/runtime#57562 and 3.1 in #43102. This PR will pave the way for backporting it to the `System.Memory` OOB package for .NET Framework.

# Customer Impact

A customer has encountered dotnet/runtime#57472 while on .NET Framework and reported it in dotnet/runtime#67295, necessiating backporting it once again. As an example provided in that issue, this memory manager:

``` csharp
private sealed class BasicMemoryManager : MemoryManager<byte>
{
    private readonly byte[] _buffer;
    public BasicMemoryManager(int size = 1024) => _buffer = new byte[size];

    protected override bool TryGetArray(out ArraySegment<byte> segment)
    {
        segment = new ArraySegment<byte>(_buffer);
        return true;
    }
    public override Span<byte> GetSpan() => _buffer;

    public override MemoryHandle Pin(int elementIndex = 0) => throw new NotSupportedException();
    public override void Unpin() => throw new NotSupportedException();
    protected override void Dispose(bool disposing) { }
}
```

will fail two of the following tests:

``` csharp
[Theory]
[InlineData(0, 32)]
[InlineData(0, 29)]
[InlineData(32, 32)] // fail: length 0
[InlineData(32, 29)] // fail: length -3
public void OffsetTest(int offset, int length)
{
    var mgr = new BasicMemoryManager(1024);
    var slice = mgr.Memory.Slice(offset, length);
    Assert.Equal(length, slice.Length);

    var ros = new ReadOnlySequence<byte>(slice);
    Assert.True(ros.IsSingleSegment);
    Assert.Equal(length, ros.Length);
}
```

# Testing

Failing test has been added in this PR.

# Risk

Low, as the change is very simple and obvious when you look at `ReadOnlySequence<T>` ctor that accepts `ReadOnlyMemory<T>` (notice line 133 versus lines 140 and 148):

https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/blob/0d40b482e6b6c232131dc808182d6b32d9d37394/src/System.Memory/src/System/Buffers/ReadOnlySequence.cs#L130-L149

Moreover, the changed code gets triggered only when creating a `ReadOnlySequence` from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by a `MemoryManager`. All other use cases (including creating from an array or from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by an array or a string) are unaffected. And the changes are identical to those in the other backports.

# Regression

This is not a regression.

With a using directive in every file `GetDeclarationDiagnostics()` on one compilation grows with roughly the square of the number of syntax trees. With one `global using` instead it stays close to linear. We ran into this through dosai, a source analyzer that puts a whole repository into one compilation: https://github.com/owasp-dep-scan/dosai/issues/65.

**Version Used**: Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp 5.9.0 from NuGet on the .NET SDK 10.0.401 with runtime 10.0.12 and Windows 11. The lines linked below are on main at bb79a699.

**Steps to Reproduce**:

1. Save the program below as `usingsbench.cs`.
2. Run `dotnet run -c Release usingsbench.cs -- 8000,16000,32000,64000`.

For each N it generates N small files. Each holds a class whose method signatures use four types from `System.Collections.Generic`. It then times `GetDeclarationDiagnostics()` on two compilations of them: one with `using System.Collections.Generic;` at the top of every file and one with a single `global using System.Collections.Generic;` instead.

```csharp
#:package Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp@5.9.0
#:property ServerGarbageCollection=true

using System.Diagnostics;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp;

var counts = args.Length > 0
    ? args[0].Split(',').Select(int.Parse).ToArray()
    : [2000, 4000, 8000, 16000];
var references = ((string)AppContext.GetData("TRUSTED_PLATFORM_ASSEMBLIES")!)
    .Split(Path.PathSeparator)
    .Where(path => Path.GetFileName(path) is "System.Runtime.dll" or "System.Collections.dll" or "System.Private.CoreLib.dll")
    .Select(path => MetadataReference.CreateFromFile(path))
    .ToList();

foreach (var count in counts)
{
    foreach (var usingsPerFile in new[] { true, false })
    {
        var trees = new List<SyntaxTree>(count + 1);
        for (var index = 0; index < count; index++)
        {
            var usingLine = usingsPerFile ? "using System.Collections.Generic;\n" : "";
            var text = usingLine
                + $"namespace App.Area{index % 100};\n"
                + $"public class C{index}\n{{\n"
                + $"    public List<int> M(List<int> values, Dictionary<string, int> map) => values;\n"
                + $"    public IEnumerable<string> N(HashSet<string> names) => names;\n"
                + "}\n";
            trees.Add(CSharpSyntaxTree.ParseText(text, path: $"C{index}.cs"));
        }
        if (!usingsPerFile)
        {
            trees.Add(CSharpSyntaxTree.ParseText("global using System.Collections.Generic;\n", path: "GlobalUsings.cs"));
        }

        var compilation = CSharpCompilation.Create(
            $"Bench{count}"
            , trees
            , references
            , new CSharpCompilationOptions(OutputKind.DynamicallyLinkedLibrary, concurrentBuild: false)
        );
        var watch = Stopwatch.StartNew();
        var errors = compilation.GetDeclarationDiagnostics().Count(d => d.Severity == DiagnosticSeverity.Error);
        watch.Stop();
        var globalDeclarations = compilation.GlobalNamespace.DeclaringSyntaxReferences.Length;
        Console.WriteLine(
            $"files {count,6} usings per file {usingsPerFile,-5}: declaration diagnostics {watch.Elapsed.TotalSeconds,7:F2} s"
            + $", errors {errors}, global namespace declarations {globalDeclarations}"
        );
    }
}
```

**Diagnostic Id**: None.

**Expected Behavior**: The time grows roughly in proportion to the number of files in both cases, as it does with the global using.

**Actual Behavior**: With a using directive in every file the time grows 4.0 and 5.7 times per doubling from 16000 files on:

```
files   8000 usings per file True : declaration diagnostics    2,24 s, errors 0, global namespace declarations 8000
files   8000 usings per file False: declaration diagnostics    0,49 s, errors 0, global namespace declarations 8001
files  16000 usings per file True : declaration diagnostics    2,88 s, errors 0, global namespace declarations 16000
files  16000 usings per file False: declaration diagnostics    0,80 s, errors 0, global namespace declarations 16001
files  32000 usings per file True : declaration diagnostics   11,63 s, errors 0, global namespace declarations 32000
files  32000 usings per file False: declaration diagnostics    1,03 s, errors 0, global namespace declarations 32001
files  64000 usings per file True : declaration diagnostics   65,97 s, errors 0, global namespace declarations 64000
files  64000 usings per file False: declaration diagnostics    2,05 s, errors 0, global namespace declarations 64001
```

Profiled with `dotnet-trace collect --profile dotnet-sampled-thread-time -- dotnet <the usingsbench.dll that dotnet run built> 32000`, the call with a using in every file spent about two thirds of its time in `SourceNamespaceSymbol.GetMatchingNamespaceDeclaration`. Over three runs the call took 14.3 to 23.4 s on the main thread and the share was 67 to 69 percent.

[`GetMatchingNamespaceDeclaration`](https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/blob/bb79a699359d8a5d4e4889e989fdf7e69268fab4/src/Compilers/CSharp/Portable/Symbols/Source/SourceNamespaceSymbol.AliasesAndUsings.cs#L69-L86) walks `_mergedDeclaration.Declarations` until it finds the one for the given syntax node. `GetUsingAliasesMap` ([line 203](https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/blob/bb79a699359d8a5d4e4889e989fdf7e69268fab4/src/Compilers/CSharp/Portable/Symbols/Source/SourceNamespaceSymbol.AliasesAndUsings.cs#L203)) and `GetUsingNamespacesOrTypes` ([line 235](https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/blob/bb79a699359d8a5d4e4889e989fdf7e69268fab4/src/Compilers/CSharp/Portable/Symbols/Source/SourceNamespaceSymbol.AliasesAndUsings.cs#L235)) reach it through `GetAliasesAndUsings` ([line 66](https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/blob/bb79a699359d8a5d4e4889e989fdf7e69268fab4/src/Compilers/CSharp/Portable/Symbols/Source/SourceNamespaceSymbol.AliasesAndUsings.cs#L66)) before they get to the cached result for that declaration. With one declaration of the global namespace per syntax tree, each of these calls for a compilation unit with usings is linear in the number of trees. #68832 fixed the same kind of walk for namespace declarations in `SyntaxTreeSemanticModel`.

Only tried with `concurrentBuild: false` on Windows.

